easily and would indeed soon pull off ourselves, 2) it's a challenge  
but doable provided someone has the necessary qualifications, 3) has  
the potential for large pay-off, and 4) if it turns out utterly  
unsuccessful not much or any harm would be done to the project itself  
beyond a lost opportunity.

> For me, a bigger priority would be stabilizing the current phylobase  
> functions and getting it ready to go to CRAN, and I don't think  
> adding Bio++ would accelerate this.

I don't think so either, but 1) this need not interfere with or delay  
achieving CRAN readiness (development could - and quite possibly  
should - occur on a branch), and 2) giving tasks of higher priority to  
a SoC student isn't the right strategy anyway - Summer of Code is much  
more a training program than it is a means of obtaining (cheap or not)  
labor to keep the project alive and moving. I.e., the benefits of SoC  
are often more long-term than having immediate pay-offs for the users  
of a project.

	-hilmar
